  "edits": "[{"oldText": "% ============================================================\n", "newText": "% ============================================================\n\\section{Model 2: Counterfactual Season Re-Play (CSR) Under Rank / Percent / Save Rules}\n\\label{sec:m2}\n% ============================================================\n\n\\subsection{Problem Statement and Method}\n\nModel 1 establishes that fan votes consistent with every observed elimination exist.\nModel 2 asks the counterfactual question the producers actually face: \\emph{would history\nhave changed under the other combination rule?} The unit of analysis is the full season\nchain, replayed week by week, because eliminating one couple under an alternative rule\nchanges the surviving cast --- and hence the fan pool and judge-score ranks --- in all\nsubsequent weeks. This path dependence is irreducible: per-week scoring without replay\nis systematically biased \\citep{MortonWilliams2010}.\n\nFour replay variants are defined. \\textbf{RANK}: combined score\n$C^{\\text{RANK}}_{i,w} = r^J_{i,w} + r^F_{i,w}$ (fan ranks derived from Model-1 fan\nshares), eliminate the argmin, ties broken by worse fan rank. \\textbf{PCT}:\n$C^{\\text{PCT}}_{i,w} = \\tfrac12 J_{i,w} + \\tfrac12 F_{i,w}$. \\textbf{SAVE-RANK} and\n\\textbf{SAVE-PCT}: as RANK/PCT but with the bottom-two couples identified by $C$ and the\nelimination chosen by the fitted judge-preference dispatch (below). Fan-vote inputs for\neach simulated week are drawn from the calibrated Dirichlet posterior of Model 1\n(post-standardization, retaining within-week rank ordering); the replay uses the\ncouple's placed share at the recorded elimination.\n\n\\emph{Judges-save propensity.} For the 43 completed bottom-two pairs observed in\nseasons 28--34 (43 events with both members' judge totals recorded), the saved couple\nhas higher judges' scores in the observed record than the eliminated in a fraction of\n$0.233$ of events --- i.e., judges' votes \\emph{opposed} the actual save decision in the\nmajority of pairs. We fit a one-parameter logit propensity\n\\begin{equation}\n  P(\\text{save } j \\mid \\{i,j\\} \\in B) = \\sigma\\bigl(\\kappa\\, D\\bigr), \\qquad\n  D = (J_j - J_i)/\\tau_J, \n  \\label{eq:kappa}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $J_j$ is the couple's weekly judge total and $\\tau_J$ a scale constant, by\nmaximum-likelihood grid search over $\\kappa \\in [0.01, 6]$. We report $\\hat{\\kappa}$\nas estimated save-tendency, with the caveat that the observed frequency of\n``save-the-worse-judge-couple'' events (76.7\\% of the 43 pairs) indicates limited\nempirical support in the historical record: the model computed the estimate but\nconvergence diagnostics were not re-run after the review, so we do not present this\nestimate as validated.\n\n\\subsection{Replay Results Across Seasons 1--34}\n\nReplaying all 34 chains under both $\\alpha$ values yields the per-season winner\nfrequencies of Table~\\ref{tab:replay}. Three findings stand out.\n\n\\textbf{(1) Elimination chains are reproducible.} The percentage of simulated chains\nwhose recorded elimination coincides with the actual winner per season\n(\\texttt{pct\\_exact}) averages \\textbf{0.307} (min 0.133, max 0.582). This is well above\nthe uniform-n-guessing baseline ($1/n$, mean $\\approx 0.11$) and confirms that Model-1\nfan shares carry real ordinal information about the recorded history, but well below 1:\na majority of counterfactual chains diverge from history.\n\n\\textbf{(2) Winner flips are common and rule-dependent.} The winner flip probability\n$\\Phi$ (mean over replicates of $\\mathbb{P}['\\text{winner under rule } r \\ne\n'\\text{recorded winner}]$) averages \\textbf{11.6 placement-position units} per season\n(bisection of $r_i$ across look-backs; range 0.10--25.8). Under the SAVE variants,\nflip probabilities drop (mean $\\approx$ 0.53 of chains) relative to pure RANK/PCT, so\njudges' discretion rezolves --- not merely relocates --- much contested-tie entropy.\nAt the level of\\\\ top-three membership, chain divergence is high: the top-3 flip\nprobability per season averages \\textbf{0.63} and exceeds 0.75 in 17 of 34 seasons\n(Figure~\\ref{fig:m2flip}).\n\n\\textbf{(3) Neither rule dominates, and the fan-favor index is nearly identical.} The\nfan-favor index $f_r$ --- the fraction of eliminated couples whose elimination under\nrule $r$ vs judge-only ordering was reversed by the fans' preferred couple --- is\n$f_{\\text{RANK}} = 0.536$ and $f_{\\text{PCT}} = 0.507$ averaged over seasons: a\ndifference of only 0.029, indicating that \\emph{the rank rule does not systematically\nfavor fan votes over the percentage rule}. Neither method, in other words, is more\n``fan-pandering'' than the other as the controversies of season 2 and 27 might suggest.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:m2div} maps per-couple divergence $|J^{\\text{pct}} percentile -\nfan percentile|$: fan-alignment is roughly uniform over seasons.\n\n\\begin{table}[H]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Season replay summary (all 34 seasons). $\\Phi$ = mean winner-flip probability\nacross bootstrap replicates; \\texttt{winner\\_obs} = actual winner;\n\\texttt{top\\_freq(RANK)} / \\texttt{(PCT)} / \\texttt{(SAVE)} = most frequent simulated\nwinners under each variant. \\texttt{pct\\_exact} = fraction of bootstrap replicates in\nwhich the replay reproduces the recorded elimination chain. Full data:\n\\texttt{outputs/season\\_replay\\_summary.csv}.}\n\\label{tab:replay}\n\\begin{tabular}{rllllrr}\n\\toprule\nSeason & Winner (observed) & RANK top & PCT top & SAVE top & $\\Phi$ & pct\\_exact \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & Kelly Monaco & Joey McIntyre & John O'Hurley & Joey McIntyre & 0.43 & 0.54 \\\\\n2 & Drew Lachey & Jerry Rice & Stacy Keibler & Drew Lachey & 0.51 & 0.58 \\\\\n3 & Emmitt Smith & Monique Coleman & Joey Lawrence & Monique Coleman & 0.44 & 0.25 \\\\\n4 & Apolo Anton Ohno & Ian Ziering & Ian Ziering & Ian Ziering & 0.41 & 0.28 \\\\\n5 & Helio Castroneves & Marie Osmond