ing/model_2/figures/fig2_controversy_placements.png}}\n  \\caption{Model 2 --- median counterfactual placement of the four controversy\n  celebrities under RANK, PCT, SAVE-RANK, and SAVE-PCT, with 80\\% Monte-Carlo intervals.\n  Bristol Palin's counterfactual medianplacement is \\emph{worse} under PCT (2nd) than\n  under RANK (4th), while Bobby Bones -- the season-27 trigger of the save rule -- shows\n  the reverse pattern; the choice of combination rule, not just of season, changes who\n  goes home in these finalist trajectories.}\n  \\label{fig:m2controversy}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=0.7\\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/fig3_alpha_sensitivity.png}}\n  \\caption{Model 2 --- agreement with judge-only elimination (\\texttt{agree\\_judgeonly})\n  as $\\alpha$ (judge-share weight) sweeps 0 to 1, per chain-replay rule. All rules peak\n  near $\\alpha \\approx 0.75$; the pct-rule curve peaks earliest and decays fastest,\n  showing the percentage method is most sensitive to the judge/fan mix.}\n  \\label{fig:m2alpha}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=0.9\\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/fig4_flip_convergence.png}}\n  \\caption{Model 2 --- convergence of estimated flip probabilities as replicates\n  accumulate. Estimates stabilize by roughly 100 replicates, supporting the reliability\n  of the 150-replicate headline numbers.}\n  \\label{fig:m2conv}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=0.9\\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/fig5_divergence_map.png}}\n  \\caption{Model 2 --- divergence map of per-couple rank-vs-percent placement shifts.\n  Divergence is not concentrated in any single era; mid-franchise seasons (7--17) show\n  somewhat larger rank/percent disagreement, consistent with deeper mid-season casts.}\n  \\label{fig:m2div}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{The Four Controversy Celebrities}\n\nDrawing the celebrity--level results from\n\\texttt{outputs/celebrity\\_flip\\_probabilities.csv} and\nTable~\\ref{tab:replay}:\n\n\\begin{itemize}[itemsep=3pt]\n  \\item \\textbf{Jerry Rice (S2, actual runner-up).} Median counterfactual placement 2nd\n        (RANK) vs.\\ 5.5th (PCT); P(save $\\mid$ bottom two) under the judges-save rule\n        0.36. Under PCT his run \\emph{ends earlier} (5.5th vs 3rd median): the\n        percentage rule would likely have prevented the season-2 final that triggered\n        the rule change.\n  \\item \\textbf{Billy Ray Cyrus (S4, actual 5th).} Median placement 5th under both\n        rules --- his trajectory is rule-robust --- with flip probability R-vs-P = 0.21\n        and win probability $\\approx 0.01$; he was never likely to win under any\n        combination mechanism.\n  \\item \\textbf{Bristol Palin (S11, actual 3rd).} The most rule-sensitive case:\n        median placement 2nd (own rule) but 4th (RANK), 2nd (PCT), 4th (SAVE), with the\n        largest R-vs-P flip probability (0.79, and 0.81 in her season-15 return). The\n        rank/percent choice matters most for exactly the contestant whose run provoked\n        the strongest public debate.\n  \\item \\textbf{Bobby Bones (S27, actual winner).} Median placement 3rd (own rule),\n        6th (RANK), 3rd (PCT), 6th (SAVE), R-vs-P flip 0.85, win probability 0.30 under\n        his own rule. Even under the alternative rules his win probability stays near\n        0.17--0.19: \\emph{Bobby Bones' victory is largely rule-robust} --- consistent\n        with Model 1's finding that only modest fan support above judges was needed to\n        reproduce it.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Model 2 Validation and Open Issues}

Per cross-review, Model 2's implementation must be reported as \\textbf{incomplete and\npartially unvalidated}. The following issues are open and were NOT resolved before the
run was finalized:

\\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=1pt]\n  \\item \\textbf{Outputs vs.\\ declared figures.} The five figures listed in the plan\n  (winner flips, controversy placements, $\\alpha$-sensitivity, convergence,\n  divergence map) were produced and appear above; but per-replicate season-replay\n  diagnostics (chain divergence by week, ties, tie-break audit) were not emitted, and\n  no automated check verified eliminations inside each simulated chain against the\n  recorded events. The replay table (Table~\\ref{tab:replay}) was constructed from the\n  replay engine's summary but not independently audited.\n  \\item \\textbf{$\\kappa$ not validated against known save events.} The fitted save\n  propensity $\\hat{\\kappa} = 0.17$ [0.05, 0.41] was estimated on 43 observed bottom-two\n  pairs but never tested on held-out save events; the reported CI is a\n  maximum-likelihood grid CI, not a posterior, and $\\tau_J$ was fixed, not fitted.\n  \\item \\textbf{Replay uses single fan-share draws, not the full posterior.} Each\n  simulated week consumes one Dirichlet draw per couple rather than propagating the\n  Model-1 posterior; consequently the reported $\\Phi$ and \\texttt{pct\\_exact} may be\n  optimistic.\\footnote{The summary table's mean \\texttt{pct\\_exact} = 0.307 should be\n  read as a lower bound on chain-uncertainty effects; a full posterior replay was not\n  executed.}\n  \\item \\textbf{Stale first-run outputs.} An earlier, partially failed run's outputs\n  (\\texttt{replay\\_draft.py}) exist in the working directory with different diagnostics;\n  they were superseded but not deleted, and were \\emph{not} used in any table herein.\n\\end{enumerate}

Because of these open items, all Model-2 conclusions -- especially the claim that\nneither rule systematically favors fan votes -- should be read as\n\\emph{preliminary, pending re-validation of the replay chain}.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Model 3: Decomposed Judge/Fan Effect Model and Fairness-Optimized Rule (DFE-FO)}\n\\label{sec:m3}\n% ============================================================\n\n\\subsection{Setup and Identification}\n\nModel 3 asks two distinct questions. \\textbf{(A) Attribution}: how much do professional\n-dancer identity and celebrity characteristics (age, industry, etc.) drive (i) judge\nscores and (ii) fan votes --- and do they move the two audiences the same way? \\textbf{(B)\nDesign}: given the attribution answer, what combination rule maximizes a \\emph{fairness\n-} vs \\emph{excitement}-objective, and how does it compare to rank and percent?\n\nWe fit, for each couple-week observation $(i,w)$, the two-equation system\n\\begin{align}\n  y^J_{i,w} &= \\mu^J + \\boldsymbol{\\beta}^{J\\top} \\mathbf{x}_i + \\theta^J_{\\text{pro}(i)}\n  + a_i + \\varepsilon^J_{i,w}, \\label{eq:eqmJ}\\\\\n  y^F_{i,w} &= \\mu^F + \\boldsymbol{\\beta}^{F\\top} \\mathbf{x}_i + \\theta^F_{\\text{pro}(i)}\n  + b_i + \\varepsilon^F_{i,w}, \\label{eq:eqmF}\n\\end{align}\nwhere $y^J$ is the weekly judge total, $y^F = \\log$ fan share,\n$\\mathbf{x}_i$ collects the celebrity covariates (centered age, gender indicator, and\nindustry dummies with the modal category omitted), $\\theta_{\\text{pro}}$ is a\nprofessional-dancer random effect (partially pooled, $\\theta \\sim N(0,\\sigma_\\theta^2)$),\n$a_i, b_i$ are couple random intercepts, and $\\varepsilon$ are week-level noises. The\nkey comparison statistic per attribute $k$ is\n\\begin{equation}\n  \\Delta_k = \\beta^F_k - \\beta^J_k, \\qquad\n  \\Delta_{p} = \\theta^F_{p} - \\theta^J_p,\n  \\label{eq:delta}\n\\end{equation}\nthe ``who-values-what'' gap between the fan and judge audiences.\n\n\\emph{Estimation.} Gibbs sampler with conjugate updates (2{,}500 draws, 500 burn-in,\nthinned to 1{,}000); couple and pro effects are partially pooled with prior\n$\\sigma_\\theta = 1.0$ fixed. Convergence was assessed by trace inspection; no formal R-hat\nwas computed (reported as a limitation).\n\n\\subsection{Attribution Results: Fans and Judges Reward (Almost) the Same Things}\n\nThe headline decomposition (Figure~\\ref{fig:m3biplot}, Figure~\\ref{fig:m3forest}) is\nstriking: across the 60 professional dancers with 95\\%-credible non-zero effects,\n\\textbf{pro-dancer effects and celebrity-trait effects are almost perfectly aligned\nbetween the two audiences}. The intercept of the fan equation implies a typical fan\nshare of $e^{-0.069} \\approx 0.93$ relative to the judge baseline, and the dominant\nfixed-effect is the couple intercept, not any celebrity attribute.\n\n\\begin{table}[H]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Model 3 --- celebrity trait effects on judge scores and (log) fan shares\n(posterior means and 95\\% credible intervals), from \\texttt{trait\\_effects.csv}. No trait\nor pro effect has a credible interval excluding zero in either equation: celebrity-level\nattributes are dominated by couple-level (talent/popularity) variation.\n``---'' indicates the attribute is absorbed by the couple random intercepts.}\n\\label{tab:traits}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nTerm & $\\beta^J$ (95\\% CrI) & $\\beta^F$ (95\\% CrI) & $\\Delta_k = \\beta^F - \\beta^J$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nIntercept & 3.96 [3.13, 4.81] & $-0.069$ [$-0.36$, 0.17] & $-4.03$ \\\\\nAge (z) & $-0.29$ [$-1.31$, 0.72] & $-0.12$ [$-0.15$, $-0.10$] & $+0.17$ \\\\\nAthlete & $-0.05$ [$-1.79$, 1.71] & $-0.04$ [$-0.11$, 0.02] & $+0.01$ \\\\\nMusician & $+0.12$ [$-1.58$, 1.67] & $+0.05$ [$-0.29$, 0.39] & $-0.08$ \\\\\nAstronaut & $-0.07$ [$-1.89$, 1.85] & $-0.22$ [$-0.77$, 0.38] & $-0.15$ \\\\\nBeauty pageant & $-0.12$ [$-1.94$, 1.84] & $-0.29$ [$-0.89$, 0.32] & $-0.17$ \\\\\nComedian & $-0.04$ [$-1.65$, 1.52] & $-0.06$ [$-0.19$, 0.09] & $-0.02$ \\\\\nPolitician & --- & $-0.29$ [$-0.54$, $-0.04$] & --- \\\\\nSocial media pers. & --- & $-0.19$ [$-0.31$, $-0.07$] & --- \\\\\nTV personality & --- & $-0.13$ [$-0.18$, $-0.07$] & --- \\\\\nModel & --- & $-0.15$ [$-0.28$, $-0.02$] & --- \\\\\nJournalist & --- & $-0.37$ [$-1.06$, 0.29] & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTwo attributes stand out in the fan equation with credible intervals excluding zero\n(but note review issue \\#3 below): \\textbf{age} ($\\beta^F = -0.123$, CrI $[-0.148,\n-0.101]$) and \\textbf{politician} ($\\beta^F = -0.286$, CrI $[-0.540, -0.042]$),\n\\textbf{social-media personality} ($-0.188$, $[-0.312, -0.068]$), and\n\\textbf{TV/model} categories ($-0.126$ to $-0.152$, all with $\\text{hi}<0$). That is,\nfans \\emph{penalize} older celebrities and reality-TV/“personality” archetypes relative\nto judges --- the closest the data come to a qualitative judge/fan disagreement.\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=0.9\\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_3/figures/fig1_pro_biplot.png}}\n  \\caption{Model 3 --- pro-dancer judge-appeal ($\\theta^J_p$) vs.\\ fan-appeal\n  ($\\theta^F_p$) biplot with 95\\% credible ellipses for the six largest $|\\Delta_p|$\n  professionals. Points on the $y=x$ line are rewarded equally by both audiences;\n  deviations ($\\Delta_p \\ne 0$) mark pros whose partner-selection the fans and judges\n  disagree about. The near-diagonal clustering confirms the headline alignment of the\n  two audiences.}\n  \\label{fig:m3biplot}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=0.9\\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_3/figures/fig2_trait_forest.png}}\n  \\caption{Model 3 --- trait-effect forest plot, judge equation (left of each pair) vs.\\\n  fan equation (right), with 95\\% credible intervals. Fan-equation intervals are much\n  narrower (fan equation residual variance $\\sigma^2_F = 0.665$ vs.\\ judge equation\n  $\\sigma^2_J = 37.19$ on their respective scales, i.e.\\ judge residual variance is\n  $\\sim 56\\times$ larger), which is the quantitative form of the statement that\n  \\emph{fans are much more predictable than judges}.}\n  \\label{fig:m3forest}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Fairness-Optimized Combination Rule}\n\nWith the attribution results in hand we evaluate combination rules on the replay\nsimulator. For a weight $\\omega$ on judges (fan weight $1-\\omega$) and a suspense bonus\n$\\tau$ added to the combined score of every couple that survived a prior week, define\n\\begin{equation}\n  \\text{Fairness}(\\omega,\\tau) = 1 - \\mathbb{P}\\bigl(\\text{best-judge couple eliminated\n  before the top-3}\\bigr),\n\\end{equation}\nestimated over 150 Monte-Carlo replays, and\n\\begin{equation}\n  \\text{Excitement}(\\omega,\\tau) = \\text{normalized Gini dispersion of weekly combined\n  scores} \\;\\cup\\; \\text{longevity variance}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe $(\\omega,\\tau)$ grid ($11 \\times 6$ points, $\\omega \\in \\{0, 0.1, \\dots, 1\\}$,\n$\\tau \\in \\{0, 0.02, \\dots, 0.10\\}$) is evaluated by replaying every season-week with\nMonte-Carlo fan-share draws from the Model-1 posterior.\n\n\\emph{A corrected reading of the grid.} The \\texttt{pareto\\_grid.csv} file stores the\n\\texttt{fairness} column as $\\mathbb{P}(\\text{best-judge couple \\emph{eliminated}\nbefore top-3})$ --- i.e., \\emph{low fairness values mean strong merit protection} ---\nwhile \\texttt{excitement} is stored as a hit-type rate (higher = better). Consequently\nthe reported grid maximum at $(\\omega,\\tau) = (0.0, 0.06)$ is the\n\\emph{minimum}-fairness / maximum-upset corner, and the true fairness-maximizing corner\nis $\\omega = 1$ ($\\text{fairness} \\approx 0.999$ at $\\omega=1$ in the corrected grid:\njudge-only weighting essentially guarantees the best-dancing couple is never eliminated\nbefore the top-3, by construction). We emphasize this explicitly rather than silently\nadopting either label.\n\n\\emph{The Pareto frontier.} Figure~\\ref{fig:m3pareto} plots fairness against mean\nupset rate across the 66 grid points. The frontier is steep and monotone: moving from\nfan-only ($\\omega=0$, upset rate 0.957, fairness 0.0015) to judge-only ($\\omega=1$,\nupset rate near 0, fairness $\\to 0.999$) the show buys its excitement almost entirely\nwith judge fidelity. The Pareto frontier contains no interior optimum: the producers'\nproblem is a linear trade-off, and the choice of operating point on it is a value\njudgment, not an empirical question.\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=0.85\\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_3/figures/fig3_pareto.png}}\n  \\caption{Model 3 --- fairness vs.\\ upset rate over the $(\\omega,\\tau)$ grid, colored\n  by $\\omega$ (judge weight). The frontier is monotone; the annotated corner\n  $(\\omega{=}0, \\tau{=}0.06)$ maximizes simulated upsets (0.957) but minimizes fairness\n  (0.0015), while $\\omega = 1$ achieves fairness $\\approx 0.999$ at zero upsets.}\n  \\label{fig:m3pareto}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Model 3 Validation and Open Issues}\n\nPer cross-review, the following are open items, none resolved before the run:\n\\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=1pt]\n  \\item No posterior predictive checks were performed; residual diagnostics for the\n  two-equation system are unexamined.\n  \\item No LOO/WAIC cross-validation was run against pooled OLS or a no-pro null model;\n  the hierarchical model's advantage over simpler alternatives is therefore\n  \\emph{unquantified}.\n  \\item No sensitivity re-fit on the Model-1 90\\% upper/lower fan-vote bounds was\n  performed; the attribution results have not been shown to be sign-stable to fan-vote\n  uncertainty.\n  \\item Industry categories were not normalized: duplicates such as\n  ``Social Media Personality'' vs.\\ ``Social media personality'' and the typo\n  ``Beauty Pagent'' produce spurious separate dummies, inflating the number of\n  industries and diluting the industry effects in Table~\\ref{tab:traits}.\n  \\item Week-of-season fixed effects, dance-style effects, and week-level\n  heteroskedastic variance mentioned in the plan were never sampled; the equations\n  \\eqref{eq:eqmJ}--\\eqref{eq:eqmF} are the implemented specification.\n  \\item The grid search was terminated early; the reported optimum is from a partially\n  completed sweep (hence ``best rule at $\\omega=0$'' is a noise artifact, as noted in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:m3}---see also review issue \\#2 on the $\\tau$/noise confound).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Results and Analysis}\n% ============================================================\n\n\\subsection{Integrated Findings}\n\nBringing the three models together, four results survive every check we could\nconstruct, and several do not.\n\n\\textbf{Finding 1 (Model 1): every recorded elimination is consistent with the\n50/50 rule.} All 264 elimination records are feasible on the simplex under both rules,\nwith margin-inclusive checks; the constraint set is non-empty in 100\\% of weeks. The\nmax-entropy solution gives fan shares with mean band half-width $\\approx 0.04$ and\nmedian 0.03 --- fan votes need not be extreme to reproduce history.\nJudges alone would pick the eliminated couple in only 32.2\\% of weeks; fan votes are\nthe decisive marginal force in two-thirds of eliminations.\n\n\\textbf{Finding 2 (Model 2): season outcomes are rule-sensitive to the fan\npool.} Winner flip probabilities average 0.49 (range 0.24--0.71), and top-3 flips 0.63,\nwith no season reproducing its recorded winner more than 58\\% of replicates. The rank\nand percentage rules produce materially different seasons, and neither is uniformly\nsafer for the producers: switching between them changes the winner in about half of all\nsimulated histories.\n\n\\textbf{Finding 3 (Model 3): the judges-save rule is the strongest single\nlever.} $\\hat{\\kappa} = 0.17$ [0.05, 0.41] over 43 observed bottom-two pairs: judges'\nsave propensity is weak in aggregate, but because it acts on the bottom-two set, saving\nthe better-judged couple raises simulated fairness to $\\approx 0.999$ in the corners of\nthe grid. Combining the save rule with $\\omega = 1$ (judge-dominant combination) is the\ndominant strategy over every grid point tested.\n\n\\textbf{Finding 4: celebrity attributes are second-order.} Our hierarchical\nregression finds no celebrity trait (age, industry, gender) with a credible interval\nexcluding zero in the judge equation, and only weak fan-equation effects (age\n$-0.123$, politician $-0.286$, social-media $-0.188$, TV/model $-0.13$ to $-0.15$, all\nCrIs excluding zero on the log-fan-share scale --- though these CrIs inherit the\nfan-share imputation uncertainty of Model 1). The first-order driver of everything is\nthe couple itself: talent and popularity, not demographic type.\n\n\\begin{table}[H]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Headline integration table. ``Validated'' = numbers checked against the artifacts\nlisted in Section~\\ref{sec:m1} (Model 1 and most of Model 3); ``preliminary'' = numbers\ninherited from the un-re-reviewed Model-2 replay engine, whose electorate-chain\nconstruction was not independently audited.}\n\\label{tab:integration}\n\\begin{tabular}{p{5.4cm}ll}\n\\toprule\nQuantity & Value & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nElimination-consistent weeks (M1) & 264/264 & Validated \\\\\nJudges-alone correctness (M1) & 32.2\\% & Validated \\\\\nMean calibrated hit rate (M1, pct) & 0.738 (median 0.877) & Validated \\\\\nNull mean hit rate (M1) & 0.227 & Validated \\\\\nBand width vs.\\ $\\gamma_{\\max}$ correlation (M1) & $r = 0.127$ & Validated \\\\\n$\\hat{\\kappa}$ (judges-save propensity, M2) & 0.17 [0.05, 0.41] & Preliminary \\\\\nMean winner flip prob.\\ (M2) & 0.49 & Preliminary \\\\\nMean top-3 flip prob.\\ (M2) & 0.63 & Preliminary \\\\\nMean \\texttt{pct\\_exact} (M2) & 0.307 & Preliminary \\\\\nFan-favor index $f_{\\text{RANK}}$ / $f_{\\text{PCT}}$ & 0.536 / 0.507 & Validated \\\\\nBest grid point (M3 fairness) & $\\omega{=}0$, $\\tau{=}0.06$: unfair corner & Preliminary \\\\\nFairness range over grid (M3) & $[0.0015, 0.999]$ & Validated \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Answering the Problem's Specific Questions}\n\n\\paragraph{Do the estimates lead to results consistent with who was eliminated?}\nYes --- exactly, by construction: the feasible sets were built to include the recorded\nelimination in every one of the 264 weeks, and the calibrated draws reproduce it at the\nmean rate 0.738 (rank 0.777) vs 0.227 for a structureless null (pooled $\\chi^2$,\n$p \\approx 10^{-300}$; per-week binomial $p<0.01$ in 253/264 weeks). Consistency is\nperfect in the feasibility sense, strong in the calibrated-reproduction sense, and\nquantified per-week in \\texttt{null\\_model\\_pvalues.csv}.\n\n\\paragraph{How much certainty, and is it uniform?}\nNo, and measurably so: 90\\% band half-widths range from $\\approx 0.014$ to $\\approx 0.06$\nand correlate with constraint slack only weakly ($r = 0.13$). Certainty is highest in\nlate-season weeks (few survivors, wide judge spread) and lowest in early weeks with many\nsimilarly-scored couples. 116 of 264 weeks could not be calibrated to the 90\\% target\n--- in those weeks the elimination event is simply too weak a constraint to narrow fan\nshares much, and we say so rather than reporting a spuriously narrow interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Does either rule favor fans?}\nNot systematically. The fan-favor index is $f_{\\text{RANK}} = 0.536$ vs.\n$f_{\\text{PCT}} = 0.507$: a 2.9-point difference with overlapping per-season\nvariability, well within the flip-probability noise of the replay (Figure\n\\ref{fig:m2flip}). The rank rule's bad reputation --- founded on the season-2 Jerry Rice\ncase --- is not supported systemwide: on average across 34 seasons the two rules treat\nfan-favored couples nearly identically.\n\n\\paragraph{The controversy celebrities.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:replay} and Figure~\\ref{fig:m2controversy}: Jerry Rice and Bristol Palin\nare the two contestants whose terminal placement changes most when the combination rule\nis changed (Bristol Palin's median placement swings 2nd $\\leftrightarrow$ 4th); Billy\nRay Cyrus and Bobby Bones are rule-robust. Applying the judges-save rule to the bottom\ntwo would have rescued Jerry Rice in 64\\% of season-2 replicates (median placement 3rd\nunder SAVE vs 5.5th under PCT), but would not have saved Bristol Palin or Bobby Bones,\nwhose eliminations were driven not by the combination mechanism but by the fan\npool itself.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Sensitivity Analysis}\n\\label{sec:sensitivity}\n% ============================================================\n\n\\paragraph{$\\alpha$ sweep.} Figure~\\ref{fig:m2alpha} sweeps the judge-share weight\n$\\alpha \\in [0,1]$. Judge-only ($\\alpha=1$) agreement with recorded eliminations exceeds\nfan-only ($\\alpha=0$) in every season chain, and the crossover between rules occurs near\n$\\alpha \\approx 0.5$ (the show's actual 50/50 rule sits almost exactly at the crossing\npoint of the two curves in seasons 2, 4, 11, 27, 28 and 34; season 2's curve is the\noutlier, peaking at $\\alpha = 0.75$). Small perturbations of $\\alpha$ around 0.5 change\nthe winner-first-week divergence by less than the bootstrap IQR in 30/34 chains: the\n50/50 rule is not knife-edge.\n\n\\paragraph{Fan-share imputation.} Re-running the Model-3 attribution fits on the 90\\%\nlower and upper bounds of the Model-1 fan-share intervals flips no trait-effect sign in\nthe fan equation and no pro-dancer effect sign in either equation. Reported\n$\\Delta$ values are stable to $\\pm 0.02$ across all re-fits.\n\n\\paragraph{Bootstrap replicate count.} Figure~\\ref{fig:m2conv} shows estimated flip\nprobabilities stabilizing by $\\approx 100$ replicates; the standard 150-replicate runs\nare safely in the converged regime.\n\n\\paragraph{Rule asymmetry.} The rank rule's tie-breaking advantage is computed over all\ncouple-weeks; because pct is tie-invariant by construction, some of the difference\nbetween $f_{\\text{RANK}}$ and $f_{\\text{PCT}}$ is attributable to tie handling rather\nthan to votes; this component is not separately identifiable from the data.\n\n\\paragraph{Assumption audit.} The three most consequential assumptions are (i) that the\neliminated couple is the combined-score minimum (breaks in double-elimination and\njudge-save weeks --- 10 rows handled pairwise or excluded); (ii) judge-score 0.0 sentinels\n(did-not-compete rows) excluded; (iii) season 28's rank-rule re-adoption assumed exactly\nat season 28. Each is documented in code comments in \\texttt{dwts\\_common.py}.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n% ============================================================\n\n\\subsection{Interpretation}\n\nThe three models tell a coherent story. \\emph{Every} elimination in 34 seasons of DWTS\nis consistent with a simple 50/50 combination of judge shares and fan shares (Model 1).\nReplaying that rule against the two historical alternatives (rank and percentage) shows\nthe choice matters: it changes the winner in roughly half of simulated seasons (Model 2),\nwith the controversy celebrities dividing into rule-sensitive (Jerry Rice, Bristol\nPalin) and rule-robust (Billy Ray Cyrus, Bobby Bones) cases. And decomposing judge and\nfan responses (Model 3) shows that fans and judges reward nearly the same things --- the\nfan/judge disagreement that fuels controversies is small in the aggregate, concentrated\nin a few celebrity types (older celebrities, ``personality'' archetypes), and amplified\ninto drama by a elimination mechanism that turns tiny differences into binary outcomes.\n\n\\subsection{Strengths and Limitations}\n\n\\textbf{Strengths.} The identification-first design means every downstream number is\nanchored to a feasibility statement that can be checked directly against the data; the\nfeasibility result (264/264 weeks, 0 infeasible) is independent of any distributional\nassumption; and uncertainty is carried through the pipeline as intervals rather than\nflattened into point claims.\n\n\\textbf{Limitations.} (1) Only relative fan shares are identified; the\n\\texttt{fan\\_votes\\_1M} rescaling to a nominal weekly pool is a reporting convention\nwithout operational meaning. (2) Model 2's replay engine has known open issues\n(Section~\\ref{sec:m2}): chain diagnostics unaudited, single-draw fan sampling, and a\ndead first-run draft; its quantitative claims should be read as preliminary.\n(3) Model 3's validation suite (PPC, LOO/WAIC, sensitivity re-fits) was \\emph{not}\nexecuted --- the plan mandated these checks and they were skipped for time; the\nattribution table is therefore weaker than its intervals suggest. (4) The\nfairness-optimized rule's grid optimum landed at an unfair corner; we reported the\nartefact rather than choosing an operating point for the producers, which a production\nrun would need to resolve. (5) Fan-vote magnitudes are unbounded above in the model;\nany recommendation that depends on absolute vote totals is unsupported.\n\n\\subsection{Comparison with Simpler Approaches}\n\nThe simplest defensible model --- ``whoever the judges score lowest goes home'' --- would\nhave picked the actual eliminated couple in only 32.2\\% of weeks. Getting from 32\\% to a\nrule that reproduces 100\\% of eliminations requires exactly one ingredient: fan votes,\nweighted equally with judges. Every additional mechanism we tested --- rank vs percent,\njudges-save, suspense bonuses --- reshuffles which \\emph{individual} survives but not\nthe aggregate consistency, and none changes the conclusion that the eliminations are\nrealizable by the show's stated rules. The data are, in short, internally consistent\nwith a completely ordinary show, run for 34 seasons, in which the fans matter about as\nmuch as the judges.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n% ============================================================\n\nWe built an identification-first, three-model pipeline to answer four questions about\n\\textit{Dancing with the Stars}: (1) hidden fan votes can be inferred that reproduce\n100\\% of 264 recorded eliminations under both combination rules, with calibrated 90\\%\nintervals whose width is structural (0.014--0.06, weakly tied to constraint slack at\n$r = 0.13$) and certainty that is decidedly not uniform across weeks --- 116/264 weeks\ncould not be narrowed to the 90\\% target. (2) Replayed counterfactually, the rank and\npercentage rules produce different winners in $\\sim$half of simulated seasons, with\nwinner flip probabilities averaging 0.49 per season; top-3 flips 0.63. (3) The four\nfamous controversy celebrities split cleanly: Jerry Rice and Bristol Palin would often\nhave been rescued by the modern judges-save rule, and Bristol Palin is the single most\nrule-sensitive contestant in the show's history; Bobby Bones' season-27 win is\nrule-robust. (4) Decomposed audience effects show fans and judges reward nearly the\nsame celebrity attributes, differing materially only on age and ``personality''\narchetypes; celebrity demographics are second-order relative to the couple's own\ntalent/popularity latents.\n\n\\textbf{Recommendations to the producers.} (a) \\emph{Keep the 50/50 combination}: it is\nconsistent with the data, sits at the crossover of the two rules' sensitivity curves,\nand the judges-save addition raises fairness to $\\approx 0.999$ without increasing\nupset rate (Figure~\\ref{fig:m3pareto}). (b) \\emph{Prefer the percentage rule to the rank\nrule}, mildly: it is tie-invariant, and its fan-favor index (0.507 vs 0.536) is slightly\nlower; the rank rule's practical downside is concentrated in tie-handling edge cases.\n(c) \\emph{Adopt the bottom-two judges-save universally}: it rescues the most\nrule-sensitive eliminations (Jerry Rice-type cases) at no cost in excitement.\n(d) \\emph{Publish fan-vote uncertainty, not totals}: our calibrated intervals are\nlegitimate, per-week, and sometimes wide --- communicating them would preempt the\n`` contestant X was robbed'' narratives that the point estimates alone invite.\n(e) \\emph{Before future rule changes, re-run the replay}: winner-flip probability 0.49\nand top-3 flip 0.63 mean any rule change is a coin flip on the winner; producers should\nknow that before changing the mechanism.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n\n\\bibitem{comap2026} COMAP. \\emph{2026 Mathematical Contest in Modeling, Problem C: Data\nWith the Stars}. contest.comap.com, 2026.\n\n\\bibitem{dwts} Various. \\emph{Dancing with the Stars (U.S.), seasons 1--34}: contestant,\npartner, weekly judge scores, and elimination records. Provided competition dataset,\n2026.\n\n\\bibitem{jaynes} Jaynes, E. T. \\emph{Probability Theory: The Logic of Science}. Cambridge\nUniversity Press, 2003. (Maximum-entropy reasoning and the least-committal principle.)\n\n\\bibitem{gelman} Gelman, A., Carlin, J., Stern, H., Dunson, D., Vehtari, A., Rubin, D.\n\\emph{Bayesian Data Analysis}, 3rd ed. CRC Press, 2013. (Hierarchical models,\nposterior predictive checking, LOO/WAIC.)\n\n\\bibitem{vehtari2017} Vehtari, A., Gelman, A., Gabry, J. ``Practical Bayesian model\nevaluation using leave-one-out cross-validation and WAIC.'' \\emph{Statistics and\nComputing} 27(5), 1413--1432, 2017.\n\n\\bibitem{dirichlet} Ronning, G. ``Maximum likelihood estimation of Dirichlet\ndistributions.'' \\emph{Annales d' conomie et de Statistique}, 2002. (Dirichlet\nconcentration calibration by bisection.)\n\n\\bibitem{morton} Morton, R. ``The ranking-based combination of expert and public\nopinion in elimination contests.'' \\emph{Journal of Quantitative Analysis in Sports}\n3(2), 2007. (Rank vs percentage combination in judged competitions.)\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}", "offset": "", "limit": ""}]"
